  {
    "insert": "The exposé proposes a study that, based on constructivist learning theory, aims to evaluate the results of leveraging LLM agents in a personalized learning approach to exam preparation by comparing a group of students using them to a control group.\nIt shows that the author has taken their steps at producing academics works and while there is room for improvement, it is clear that much effort was put into the exposé and with some refinements, this has the potential to become an exposé of high quality.\n\nLiterature research is an important part of every academic work, with finding relevant and high-quality papers being an important skill taught in this course.\nThe papers you reference are all very recent and relevant to your area of research, with most of them being of decent quality, so you clearly put some great thoughts into your paper selection.\nThe only paper of low quality that I noticed is reference 8, which is published by Scientific Research Publishing, a publisher with a reputation for being predatory. You should look into replacing this reference with one of better quality, which will help you with further evaluating the quality of your sources and aid you with perfecting your literature research skills.\n\nAn important task of an exposé is identifying a research gap and deriving research questions (RQs) from it. This is important for stating the relevance of your work, positioning it against the current SOTA and guiding your study design.\nYour motivation fails to point out a research gap and does not contain RQs; Both of these should be included so that the novelty of your work becomes clear.\nYou should further engage with the current literature on the topic of LLMs for learning in order to figure out a research gap to derive your RQs from, especially meta-analyses can be of help here, as they summarize current work and therefore usually point out where gaps might lie.\n\nAnother goal of this course is learning how to conduct proper scientific work, which entails adhering to proper citation/quotation styles. While your citations fine, your quotations are insufficiently recognizable as such, which might lead to accusations of plagiarism.\nExamples for these quotations include your hook, the first sentence of your motivation and your introduction of constructivist learning theory, which classify as plagiarism in their current form.\nYou are most likely aware that plagiarism is unacceptable in an academic context and I hope your intention with including the reference was to mark the sentence as a quotation. If that was your intention, you should follow a quotation style such as the APA style [1]. However, it is generally advised not to use quotations, opting to paraphrase instead, and especially your hook should be written by yourself so that you can potentially include claims from multiple sources and already begin leading into your research gap using it.\nYou should therefore write the hook yourself, referencing any sources you may need to back up claims made in it, and fix the other quotations so that they adhere to a previously chosen style, though it might be best to paraphrase them instead, allowing you to better integrate them into your own text.\n\nWriting an exposé requires doing some literature research and then leveraging the findings in the final product properly. Finding relevant literature and good references is crucial for establishing a good SOTA and the cited papers should not be misrepresented or misused. Lectures in this course pointed out how to decide on the quality and relevance of a given paper and any claims that are not trivial or well-known should be backed by a reference.\nReference 1 does not refer to which academic definition of personalized learning they adhere, but this is a relevant problem as Bernacki et al. point out [2]. Reference 3 is a blog post by a company which has a direct financial incentive in trying to sell you on the theory (there is a literal \"buy\" button at the bottom of the webpage); You should consider citing some academic work which introduces this concept instead.\nThe exposé also suffers from missing references, especially in the motivation. Important claims such as an \"AI-based program\" being able to improve learning outcomes are not backed by references when they should be, this would also help in positioning your work with regards to the current SOTA.\nFurthermore, reference 5 seems to be misrepresented in its only use as it does not discuss computational cost, this should be corrected.\nIn order to further train your literature research skills, you should replace your references to blog posts with academic papers. The papers you reference are generally pretty good, so I am positive that finding more papers of similar quality and relevance should not be an issue. I further recommend including literature reviews in your references as those usually do a great job of summarizing the existing SOTA, which might help in improving your own SOTA section, see my annotations for more information on specific improvements or where references might be needed.\n\nDeveloping a good study approach to answer one's research questions is integral to a good exposé; It's methodology should be clearly defined and it's goals should logically follow from it.\nConducting a controlled experiment is a good way to test the capabilities of the LLM agents. However, a few important details are missing. First of all, it is unclear where you will get the agent from. If you are aiming to test an already-existing solution, you should name it. Developing a model from scratch and then testing it is probably out of scope for a bachelor's thesis and your schedule does not include this task. Therefore, you should name the tools you will provide to the students. The lack of clarity in the agents leads to another question: What are these agents helping with? There are lots of things an agent could do, such as developing the learning plans, generating tasks for the students to solve, or acting as a way to look up information. It should be clear what capabilities the agent has and how they relate to your research questions. Another problem might arise from your testing group. How will you recruit the students, from where, and what grade are you aiming for? Experiences in using such an agent may vary wildly across these factors, and you should control them in order to get more precise results.\nIn order to achieve this course's goal of learning how to design studies well, you should be more precise in your study planning. Before designing the study, you should define your research questions, but further engaging with the study design might show some issues with those, helping you there as well.\n\n"
  },
